@inherits LayoutComponentBase

@* ── CHROME ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Wrapped in <UpChrome>, so when Unpoly requests a fragment (.content) the
   header and footer are not rendered at all. Their child components are never
   initialised, so their queries never run.
   Open the Network tab and compare the two response sizes to see it.
   📖 https://unpoly.com/optimizing-responses
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────*@
@* Provides: .site-nav lives in here, so a request targeting it must get the chrome back.
   Without this, [up-target=".content, .site-nav"] swaps nothing and reports nothing. *@
<UpChrome Provides=".site-nav, .site-header">
    <header class="site-header">
        <h1 class="brand"><a href="/">JUBIN</a></h1>
        <p class="tagline">The perfect wardrobe for petite girls</p>

        @* [up-nav] keeps the .up-current class in sync with the current URL,
           even though this nav is never swapped itself.
           📖 https://unpoly.com/navigation-bars *@
        <nav class="site-nav" up-nav>
            <a href="/shop">Tất cả</a>
            @foreach (var c in Catalog.Categories)
            {
                <a href="/shop/@c.Slug">@c.Name</a>
            }
            <a href="/login">Đăng nhập</a>
            <a href="/lab">Lab</a>
            <a href="/receipt">Hoá đơn</a>

        </nav>
    </header>
</UpChrome>

@* Not wrapped: this IS the target, so it must always be present. *@
<main class="content">
    @Body
</main>

@* An EMPTY [up-flashes] container, and empty on purpose.

   The layout cannot render the message itself: Blazor builds the layout's render tree --
   including everything after @Body -- before the child page's form handler runs, so a flash
   set by that handler is always one render too late here. Moving the markup below @Body does
   not help; the ordering is in the render pass, not the document.

   Pages render their own [up-flashes] with content. An empty one does not clear existing
   messages, so this is safe on every page. 📖 https://unpoly.com/flashes *@
<div class="flashes" up-flashes></div>

@* OUTSIDE UpChrome, and that is the whole point.

   [up-hungry] updates this from any response that CONTAINS it, and Unpoly does not add
   hungry selectors to X-Up-Target -- so UpChrome's Provides never fires for it. Inside the
   chrome the badge is stripped from exactly the fragment responses that would have updated
   it, and the update silently finds nothing.

   It also needs an [id]. Unpoly derives a target selector from [up-id] or [id], not from an
   arbitrary class -- with only a class it cannot name the element, so it cannot swap it.

   And it is rendered AFTER @Body. The layout's markup above @Body is built before the page's
   form handler runs, so the badge would show the count from before the click -- it swaps
   correctly and shows a stale number, which looks exactly like [up-hungry] not working.

   Three rules, all learned the hard way: a hungry element may never live inside skippable
   chrome, it must carry a derivable selector, and in Blazor it must render after @Body if it
   reflects anything a page handler did.
   📖 https://unpoly.com/target-derivation
   📖 https://unpoly.com/up-hungry *@
<a href="/cart" id="cart-badge" class="cart-badge" up-hungry>Giỏ (@Cart.Count)</a>

<UpChrome>
    <footer class="site-footer">
        This chrome disappears from fragment responses. That is the point of the exercise.
    </footer>
</UpChrome>
